      {
        "id": "p7-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "For each natural number $n\\in\\mathbb N$, let $A_n=\\left(0,\\frac1n\\right)$. Which numbers belong to every one of these sets?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p7-q11-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "None.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p7-q11-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Every positive number is excluded once ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\frac1n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is sufficiently small; 0 is always excluded.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Every member must be positive because $A_n=\\left(0,\\frac1n\\right)$. For any $x>0$, choose a natural number $n>\\frac1x$. Then $\\frac1n<x$, so $x\\notin A_n$. Also, $0$ is excluded from every $A_n$. Therefore no number belongs to every set.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "For each natural number $n\\in\\mathbb N$, let $B_n=\\left[0,\\frac1n\\right]$. Which numbers belong to every one of these sets?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p7-q11-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Only 0.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p7-q11-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "0 is included in every interval; every positive number is eventually excluded.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Since $0\\le0\\le\\frac1n$ for every natural number $n$, $0$ belongs to every $B_n$. Negative numbers belong to none of them. For any $x>0$, choose a natural number $n>\\frac1x$. Then $\\frac1n<x$, so $x\\notin B_n$. Therefore the only common member is $0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
